WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.770
- [Narrator] When people clock into work,

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.810
economists are watching.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.400
Going back to the 1950s,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.530
the change in the number
of hours people work

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.570
has been a warning sign.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.670
Before almost every recession,

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.631
that number has dropped,

00:00:13.631 --> 00:00:16.560
and today it's happening again.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.800
- The average number of
hours worked each week

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.800
has declined from pretty
high levels to 2019 levels

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.800
and even maybe a little bit below.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:29.730
- [Narrator] But this time,

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.320
economists aren't so sure

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.470
this dip means the U.S.
is headed for a recession.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.200
Here's why hours worked
is a recession indicator,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.963
and why this slowdown could be different.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.920
Hours worked is basically
what it sounds like,

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.330
the amount of hours per week

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.958
that private sector employees punch in.

00:00:48.958 --> 00:00:51.960
Since businesses are more
likely to adjust hours

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.810
before hiring or laying off workers,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.440
these changes can signal
increases or decreases

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.990
in overall economic activity.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.180
- But if the consumers are spending less,

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.860
spending less, spending less,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.960
companies are gonna start
to feel that pressure.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.763
They're going to first start to cut hours,

00:01:09.763 --> 00:01:11.290
if they have that ability,

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.050
because they don't
really wanna cut workers.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.870
- [Narrator] Economists have
been looking at it for decades.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.220
A scholar in the 1980s

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.320
said cutting hours in manufacturing

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.131
was so cyclically consistent

00:01:21.131 --> 00:01:24.062
that it was a major leading indicator.

00:01:24.062 --> 00:01:25.770
But despite this history,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.280
some think this usually
reliable recession indicator

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.650
could be a false alarm this time

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.223
because unusual post-pandemic
factors are at work.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.890
Today in the private sector,

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.370
manufacturing,
transportation, construction,

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:46.080
retail and hospitality
working hours are going down.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.490
The overall average declined
to about 34 hours in May.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.660
That's down from a peak of
35 hours in January, 2021,

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.530
and is below the 2019
average before the pandemic.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.363
- It's hard to argue

00:01:59.363 --> 00:02:01.620
that the economy is struggling
at this current moment.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.600
The concern, and I guess

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.790
which is always the
million dollar question,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.980
is why can't you predict a
recession or anything like that?

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.120
And that's because the turning point,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.650
it happens very fast.

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.224
- [Narrator] Growth has begun to slow

00:02:12.224 --> 00:02:14.640
or turn negative by one measure,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.880
and some employers may be
responding by cutting hours,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.100
like engine company American Fleet.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.900
- They had a huge overtime
bill in 2021 and 2022.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.640
They were just like,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.985
were flooded with so much
demand they couldn't keep up,

00:02:28.985 --> 00:02:32.781
and now they've cut all
those overtime hours

00:02:32.781 --> 00:02:35.310
and they're having their workers

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.020
do sort of like random other stuff

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.900
and preparing for
business to pick up again.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.000
But yeah, no overtime hours anymore.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.220
- [Narrator] The company said
it isn't planning layoffs

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.400
and it'd do everything
it could to keep workers.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.760
And other employers are
cutting regular hours too.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.860
In June, the number of part-time workers

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.120
who want to work full-time jumped 452,000,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.490
the biggest increase in three years.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.782
- First thing they're gonna
do is kinda cut back hiring,

00:03:01.782 --> 00:03:03.900
and cut back hiring may necessarily

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.700
also be cutting back
available opportunities

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.110
for part-time work.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.240
And then the next piece
would be cutting back hours

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.520
within the employees
that work there already.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.500
And then the final piece
is letting them go.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.660
- But while employers are cutting hours,

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.610
they're also adding workers,

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:21.000
something they don't usually
do when layoffs are ahead.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.470
In the first 1/2 of this year,

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:27.470
U.S. employers added an average
of 278,000 jobs each month.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.420
And in May, there were 14% fewer layoffs

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.450
than in the average month in 2019.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.430
- This has been the
toughest hiring environment

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.780
we've ever seen,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:40.050
and we do not wanna be laying people off.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:44.070
Nah, we're just gonna hang onto
them and ride through this.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.040
- [Narrator] By riding out
tough times with more employees,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.190
economists think workers
can stop overworking

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.590
and return to manageable hours.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.770
And one study from Washington
University in St. louis

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.126
suggests many people are working less

00:03:58.126 --> 00:04:00.150
because they want to.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.472
- Maybe expectations
about jobs have changed,

00:04:02.472 --> 00:04:05.430
expectations about work-life balance,

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.420
working from home.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.830
I think those are conversations

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.080
that we're still trying to
work out in the labor market.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.540
- [Narrator] Taking the post
pandemic factors into account,

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:16.650
the hours worked indicator
may defy history this time.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.240
- Those make it seem

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.682
that the decline we're seeing right now

00:04:20.682 --> 00:04:24.449
is maybe not as ominous
as it would normally be.

00:04:24.449 --> 00:04:27.032
(bright music)

